1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,980
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:05,760
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:12,020
My name is Catherine and today we're going to continue in our series about job interviews.

4
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:12,960
That's right.

5
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:21,550
And on this occasion we're taking a look at part five of our interview series where we explain why we left our previous job.

6
00:00:22,010 --> 00:00:25,050
Okay, and so in an interview, this is a very popular question.

7
00:00:25,390 --> 00:00:26,690
Why did you leave your last job?

8
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:29,890
Learn some tools for answering this question in today's dialogue.

9
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:33,130
We're going to take a listen to it first and we'll be back in a moment.

10
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:42,670
Okay, now I'd like to find out more about your last job.

11
00:00:43,170 --> 00:00:45,890
I see you spent almost four years at the London Weekly.

12
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:47,160
Is that right?

13
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:47,710
Yes, that's right.

14
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:50,560
To be honest, the first year was quite tough for me.

15
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:52,900
I was really just treated more like an intern.

16
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:56,300
I didn't have many responsibilities and I found it quite frustrating.

17
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:58,240
So, what changed?

18
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:04,260
Well, slowly but surely I proved myself and the new editor liked me so he promoted me to Features Writer.

19
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:06,180
Wow, a real step up.

20
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:09,560
Yes, I was responsible for restaurant and food reviews mostly.

21
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:11,600
I spent three years in that position.

22
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:16,120
But to be honest, it wasn't an area of journalism I wanted to stay in long term.

23
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:17,040
I see.

24
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,400
So why did you decide to leave finally?

25
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,800
I just felt that the paper couldn't offer me any new opportunities.

26
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:26,140
I really needed a more challenging role to be honest.

27
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:43,940
Alright, we're back and as we saw, Rebecca handled it quite well explaining why you left your last job because obviously you don't want to say something like, I just really didn't like my company or the boss was a really bad guy.

28
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:45,600
Or I was fired for being lazy.

29
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:46,340
Exactly.

30
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:50,380
So, why don't we take a look at a couple of different words on Language Takeaway.

31
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:55,120
Language Takeaway.

32
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:58,770
Okay, so we've got some great descriptive words here.

33
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:00,480
These are some adjectives.

34
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:02,110
The first one is tough.

35
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:07,550
So Rebecca said, to be honest, the first year was quite tough for me.

36
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,960
Alright, so she's describing that her first year at that company where she was working, it was tough and that means hard, difficult.

37
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:16,960
That's right.

38
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:18,980
You could say, this is a really tough situation.

39
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:21,040
That means it's a difficult situation.

40
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:23,280
It's hard because there's no easy answer.

41
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:23,860
Exactly.

42
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:29,080
So, if you describe something as tough, it means that it's very difficult.

43
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:32,740
Now there is another meaning to this where it can be that you are a strong person.

44
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:33,540
Right.

45
00:02:33,540 --> 00:02:36,340
So that's different because we're talking about a person, not a situation.

46
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:45,840
But if you're talking about a job or an experience or even homework, like this homework is really tough, it means that it's very hard.

47
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:46,840
Exactly.

48
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:52,880
So apart from her job being a little bit tough, she said that it was also a little bit frustrating.

49
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:55,770
Okay, so usually these things go together.

50
00:02:56,170 --> 00:03:00,370
If something is tough, like it's difficult, it's usually frustrating too.

51
00:03:01,130 --> 00:03:08,630
So in this case, something is frustrating when it makes you unhappy or for example, it makes you feel stressed.

52
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:12,210
So something that's frustrating is not a good thing.

53
00:03:12,510 --> 00:03:12,790
Exactly.

54
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:24,180
So as you gave the example with the homework assignment, if you have maybe a math assignment that's very difficult and you can't solve it, you don't find the solution, then you get frustrated.

55
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:29,600
You get maybe angry at yourself or you get very angry for not being able to solve it.

56
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,680
So that's when something is frustrating.

57
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:33,520
I hate being frustrated.

58
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:35,200
Exactly.

59
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:37,120
Well, it's not a nice feeling.

60
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:43,840
But she said that, well, it was frustrating, it was tough, but after a while, I proved myself.

61
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:53,220
Okay, so that means that she was able to show someone that she can handle difficult things, difficult work, difficult situations.

62
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:58,080
And so she said, I proved myself and the new editor liked me.

63
00:03:58,090 --> 00:04:00,130
So basically, she did a really good job.

64
00:04:00,390 --> 00:04:00,670
Exactly.

65
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:08,930
So if you prove yourself, you're showing someone that you have great ability or capabilities of solving a problem or being able to handle a difficult job.

66
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:09,590
That's right.

67
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:15,920
So you could say, last year, Bob didn't do very well at school, but this year, he really proved himself.

68
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,640
He studied hard and he got really good grades.

69
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:19,640
Exactly.

70
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:24,360
And well, Rebecca, what she got in exchange for proving herself, she got promoted.

71
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:25,120
This is great.

72
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,360
So promotions are good things.

73
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:31,000
The verb here is to promote or to be promoted.

74
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:33,680
So in this case, she was promoted.

75
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:39,360
You could say, last year, I was promoted or he will be promoted next week.

76
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:40,220
He doesn't know yet.

77
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:41,880
Exactly.

78
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:46,520
Now, remember that to be promoted means to get a better position in your job.

79
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:55,120
Now, as we mentioned, the verb is to promote and you may be asking yourself why we are using the verb to be in the past, was promoted.

80
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:58,400
And this is because it's an ed adjective.

81
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:00,780
It's an adjective that ends in ed.

82
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:03,190
So you may think it's a verb, but it's not.

83
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:04,760
It's actually an adjective.

84
00:05:04,770 --> 00:05:05,710
The verb here is to be.

85
00:05:06,190 --> 00:05:06,530
Exactly.

86
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:08,400
So I was promoted.

87
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:15,280
So was is obviously our verb here, the verb to be, but promoted is an adjective that ends in ed.

88
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:15,680
Okay.

89
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:20,740
And so if I'm the boss, the action I do, the verb I do is I promote someone.

90
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:27,700
So this can be a little bit tricky, but we recommend that you check out the website for more details because we have lots of examples of how to use this.

91
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:28,480
Exactly.

92
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:39,520
Well, she got promoted and well, she was really happy for a while, but then she said, you know what, I really needed a more challenging role, a more challenging job.

93
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:39,940
Okay.

94
00:05:40,520 --> 00:05:41,200
Challenging is a great adjective.

95
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:45,200
It means something is difficult or it gives you a challenge.

96
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:46,360
So that means it's not easy.

97
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:47,960
Yeah.

98
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:50,220
So she found that her job was a little bit too easy.

99
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:52,320
So she wanted something a little bit more challenging.

100
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:53,140
Okay.

101
00:05:53,260 --> 00:06:00,290
And so this is a really great word for job interviews in particular, because you're basically saying, I want hard work.

102
00:06:00,750 --> 00:06:02,310
I want to prove myself.

103
00:06:02,690 --> 00:06:03,230
There we go again.

104
00:06:03,760 --> 00:06:07,200
So I want to show you that I'm a good employee or a hard worker.

105
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,370
And so I want a challenge, something that's hard.

106
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:11,040
Exactly.

107
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:16,360
So something that is hard is a challenge or you want something challenging.

108
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:19,050
So why don't we review these words that we've taken a look at now.

109
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:21,950
Let's go back to the dialogue and listen to it again.

110
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:29,080
Okay.

111
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,060
Now I'd like to find out more about your last job.

112
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:35,320
I see you spent almost four years at the London Weekly.

113
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:36,100
Is that right?

114
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:37,120
Yes, that's right.

115
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:39,980
To be honest, the first year was quite tough for me.

116
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:42,300
I was really just treated more like an intern.

117
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:45,660
I didn't have many responsibilities and I found it quite frustrating.

118
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:47,640
So what changed?

119
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:52,000
Well slowly but surely I proved myself and the new editor liked me.

120
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,660
So he promoted me to features writer.

121
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:54,380
Wow.

122
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:55,620
A real step up.

123
00:06:55,970 --> 00:06:56,230
Yes.

124
00:06:56,250 --> 00:06:58,650
I was responsible for restaurant and food reviews.

125
00:06:58,650 --> 00:07:01,010
Mostly I spent three years in that position.

126
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:05,530
But to be honest, it wasn't an area of journalism I wanted to stay in long term.

127
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:06,450
I see.

128
00:07:06,890 --> 00:07:08,770
So why did you decide to leave finally?

129
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:12,170
I just felt that the paper couldn't offer me any new opportunities.

130
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:15,530
I really needed a more challenging role to be honest.

131
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:19,480
All right, we're back.

132
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:28,240
So now let's take a look at a couple of phrases we picked out for you on Fluency Builder.

133
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:28,570
Fluency Builder.

134
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:34,370
Okay, this first phrase is unusual because it actually happens at the end of sentences.

135
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:38,930
This is very, very common with people who are making small talk or who are doing interviews.

136
00:07:39,810 --> 00:07:42,430
This is the phrase, is that right?

137
00:07:42,990 --> 00:07:50,850
So basically you saw that in the first line Mr. Parsons was talking to her and said, well, I see you spent almost four years at the London Weekly.

138
00:07:51,210 --> 00:07:51,750
Is that right?

139
00:07:52,370 --> 00:07:55,360
So he's ending the sentence with this phrase.

140
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:57,780
So he's making the sentence a question.

141
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:00,400
I see that you did this.

142
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:00,920
Is that right?

143
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:07,780
So he's asking her to confirm the fact that he knows about her because maybe they've never really talked before.

144
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:12,420
So this is a good way for him to ask her to give more details.

145
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:17,360
So for example, Marco, I hear that you're from Ecuador.

146
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:17,700
Is that right?

147
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:18,740
That's right.

148
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:24,960
So apart from ending the sentence like this, if maybe somebody asks you, is that right?

149
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:26,220
You have to confirm.

150
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:28,020
You have to say, yes, that's right.

151
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:29,140
Or yes.

152
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:32,240
It's also good to elaborate, to give more details.

153
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:34,480
So yes, I'm from Ecuador.

154
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:36,940
I was born there, but I've lived in the United States.

155
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:41,400
This is a way to give more information because that's basically what the person's asking.

156
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:46,880
Yeah, I think it wouldn't be very good if you're at a job interview and the person says, is that right?

157
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:48,000
And you say, yes, that's right.

158
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:49,640
And then you don't say anything else.

159
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:54,470
So yeah, basically the point is you have to confirm or deny.

160
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:57,040
You have to say, yes, that's right or no, that's not right.

161
00:08:57,040 --> 00:08:58,910
And then you have to give some information about the question.

162
00:08:59,190 --> 00:08:59,410
Exactly.

163
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:01,560
All right.

164
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:10,930
Now, moving on, when Rebecca again was talking about her job, she was saying it's tough and I didn't have many responsibilities and I found it quite frustrating.

165
00:09:11,530 --> 00:09:11,830
Okay.

166
00:09:11,830 --> 00:09:13,290
So this is like lost and found.

167
00:09:13,690 --> 00:09:15,490
Like I lost my hat and I found it later.

168
00:09:15,730 --> 00:09:16,550
No, no.

169
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:19,390
This is what's interesting about this verb, find.

170
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:25,210
We are not actually looking for something or we didn't actually find something we were looking for.

171
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:27,810
This is kind of a realization, right?

172
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:29,440
That's right.

173
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:29,810
It's like a feeling.

174
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:33,210
So I find this movie boring.

175
00:09:33,880 --> 00:09:35,690
That means I think this movie is boring.

176
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:38,550
It's the way that you're thinking about something.

177
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:39,960
That's right.

178
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:42,790
Or you can say, oh, I found Paris to be quite an expensive city.

179
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:44,240
Yeah, pretty expensive.

180
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:46,320
So this is your impression.

181
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:48,700
This is the way that you feel about something.

182
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:49,120
Exactly.

183
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:52,980
So this is another way that you can use this word to find.

184
00:09:53,600 --> 00:10:04,120
Now again, when Rebecca talked about proving herself, she used a very interesting, I would say phrase, a very popular well-known phrase, slowly but surely.

185
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:05,480
Okay.

186
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:09,100
So slowly but surely is the opposite of right away.

187
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:10,520
Right?

188
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:13,260
So something that happens right away is immediate.

189
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:15,020
Like I got promoted right away.

190
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:22,320
But slowly but surely means after lots of time and even hard work, this good thing happened.

191
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:24,340
Or it's usually a good thing, actually.

192
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:26,680
Yeah.

193
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:32,560
You're basically saying that you didn't do anything very quickly, but you did it very well.

194
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:36,480
So slowly but surely, I finished building my home.

195
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:37,180
Right.

196
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:40,280
So it didn't happen overnight, but it did happen over time.

197
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:42,500
You can also say this about studying a language.

198
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:48,360
At the beginning, learning English was really hard, but slowly but surely, I became fluent.

199
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:49,260
Exactly.

200
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:51,160
Okay.

201
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:52,960
So slowly but surely.

202
00:10:52,960 --> 00:11:03,180
And the last phrase that we have for you today, you may have seen that Rebecca used this phrase, to be honest, a couple of times before explaining something.

203
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:05,640
So why does she say to be honest?

204
00:11:06,260 --> 00:11:11,340
To be honest is sometimes something we say in English to fill some space or some time.

205
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,280
We call it filler.

206
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:15,240
But it's also a way to introduce something that's negative.

207
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:20,400
So for example, in this dialogue, she's talking about her negative experiences.

208
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:22,100
My job was tough.

209
00:11:22,320 --> 00:11:23,340
My experience was bad.

210
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:28,340
So she says, to be honest, it wasn't the best experience, but I learned from it.

211
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:32,280
She's saying this to introduce some things that are maybe not positive, not happy things.

212
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:32,900
That's right.

213
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:36,400
So it's another way of saying, look, I'm going to tell you the truth.

214
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:40,880
It may not be something positive, but this is what happened.

215
00:11:40,880 --> 00:11:45,820
So to be honest, I didn't really like my last employer or something like that.

216
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:46,880
Right.

217
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:47,880
We can even talk about this with your friends.

218
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:50,240
So hey, Marco, did you like that movie Shutter Island?

219
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:53,070
To be honest, I didn't really see it.

220
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:54,320
Oh.

221
00:11:54,320 --> 00:11:55,320
I fell asleep.

222
00:11:55,320 --> 00:11:57,110
Or you could say, to be honest, I didn't love it.

223
00:11:57,310 --> 00:11:58,170
I didn't really like it.

224
00:11:58,590 --> 00:11:58,990
Exactly.

225
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:04,280
So it's a very good phrase, but don't overuse it.

226
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:21,600
I think sometimes you get into the habit of using it all the time and just like, like, you know, well, you know, like I went to the movies like yesterday, like, and like, to be honest, like, so try to keep these fillers as you described them to a minimum.

227
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:22,850
So don't really use them too much.

228
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:24,120
Yes.

229
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:24,470
Say thoughts and ideas.

230
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:31,590
Nice action verbs, not too much fillers, because sometimes, especially in an interview, it makes people think that you don't really know much to say.

231
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:32,310
Yeah, exactly.

232
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:34,040
Okay.

233
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:37,050
So listen to the dialogue for the last time and we'll be back to chat a little bit more.

234
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:44,860
Okay.

235
00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:47,840
Now I'd like to find out more about your last job.

236
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:51,100
I see you spent almost four years at the London Weekly.

237
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:51,880
Is that right?

238
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:52,900
Yes, that's right.

239
00:12:53,680 --> 00:12:55,760
To be honest, the first year was quite tough for me.

240
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:58,080
I was really just treated more like an intern.

241
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:01,480
I didn't have many responsibilities and I found it quite frustrating.

242
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:03,440
So what changed?

243
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:09,440
Well slowly but surely I proved myself and the new editor liked me so he promoted me to Features Writer.

244
00:13:09,980 --> 00:13:11,400
Wow, a real step up.

245
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:14,740
Yes, I was responsible for restaurant and food reviews mostly.

246
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:17,680
I spent three years in that position.

247
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:21,320
But to be honest, it wasn't an area of journalism I wanted to stay in long term.

248
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:22,240
I see.

249
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:24,560
So why did you decide to leave finally?

250
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:27,960
I just felt that the paper couldn't offer me any new opportunities.

251
00:13:28,780 --> 00:13:31,320
I really needed a more challenging role to be honest.

252
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:42,720
Alright so talking about your last employer, this is a pretty ideal thing.

253
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:48,720
Now do you have any advice that you can give our listeners about describing your last job?

254
00:13:48,940 --> 00:13:50,560
Why did you leave your previous employer?

255
00:13:51,170 --> 00:13:53,750
Well I do have a little bit of basic job advice.

256
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:57,640
I would say don't complain about your last employer.

257
00:13:57,640 --> 00:14:10,530
Don't say my boss was mean, my co-workers were lazy, my company was terrible because usually as a person doing an interview, that makes you think that this person, if they work for you, will complain about you.

258
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:14,630
And so I think it's important like what Rebecca does in this dialogue to keep it positive.

259
00:14:15,640 --> 00:14:18,560
Every negative situation can become a positive experience.

260
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:23,120
So the job was tough but it was a good challenge, right?

261
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:25,920
Or but she learned how to do many new things.

262
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:37,880
I think this is important for anybody in any language but in English especially to use some of these phrases and to say, you know, I really like a challenge or I didn't feel this was challenging enough, things like this.

263
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:44,710
As opposed to, my old boss is so mean, he made me work on the weekends, that company is so terrible.

264
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:46,800
I mean that kind of stuff no one wants to hear.

265
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:58,130
Yeah exactly and I think it's not really useful because even if the company is the competitor, so you don't really get any extra points for giving them like the dirty secrets of what was going on in the company.

266
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:03,190
Maybe saying, oh my co-workers, they were just so mean to me and stuff like this.

267
00:15:03,770 --> 00:15:08,430
Yeah, no, it makes you seem like a complainer and no one wants to hire a complainer.

268
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:10,000
Exactly.

269
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,250
Alright so this is an interesting topic we would like to hear from you.

270
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:18,450
So come to EnglishPod.com and tell us why did you leave your previous employer?

271
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:23,450
Or just let us know about some of your interview experiences or some of your interview questions.

272
00:15:23,910 --> 00:15:24,830
We're happy to help you out.

273
00:15:24,970 --> 00:15:26,130
We'll hope to see you at the website.

274
00:15:26,430 --> 00:15:27,050
Alright we'll see you there.

275
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:28,480
Bye.
